# PASSWD(1)

## NAME

passwd - change login password

## SYNOPSIS

| **passwd** [ name ]

## DESCRIPTION

This command changes (or installs) a password associated with the user *name* (your own name by
default).

The program prompts for the old password and then for the new one. The caller must supply both.
The new password must be typed twice, to forestall mistakes.

**Note:** the old password is not asked for when the account has none - which is how every
account on this system but **root** ships - nor when the caller is the super-user.

**Note:** with no argument the name comes from getlogin(3), which reads the /etc/utmp record
for *this terminal* rather than mapping the user id. A caller who has used su(1) is therefore
asked about the account they logged in as.

New passwords must be at least four characters long if they use a sufficiently rich alphabet
and at least six characters long if monocase. These rules are relaxed if you are insistent
enough.

Only the owner of the name or the super-user may change a password; the owner must prove he
knows the old password. *Passwd* is set-user-id to root, which is how it comes to be able to
write /etc/passwd at all; the check above is made against the *real* user id.

**Note:** the file is rewritten by copying it through /etc/ptmp and copying that back over
/etc/passwd, there being no rename(2) on this system. /etc/passwd keeps its i-number, so
nothing holding it open sees it vanish - but a crash between the two copies leaves it short.

**Note:** /etc/ptmp is also the lock, and it leaks. Its existence is what makes a second
*passwd* say `Temporary file busy`, and the error paths after it is created do not remove it -
so an interrupted run leaves every later one refusing. Remove it by hand. This is v7's
behaviour and is left alone deliberately: the alternative is unlinking a file this process may
not have created.

## DIAGNOSTICS

Exit status is 0 when the password was changed and 1 otherwise.

**Note:** v7 exited 1 in both cases, so no script could tell the two apart.

## FILES

| /etc/passwd
| /etc/ptmp
| /etc/utmp

## SEE ALSO

| login(1), su(1), newgrp(1), passwd(5), crypt(3), getlogin(3)
| Robert Morris and Ken Thompson, *Password Security: A Case History*
